# CMS Auth & Permissions

Secure by default. Admin and APIs never rely on UI hiding alone.

## Basics

- ASP.NET Core Identity (or equivalent) for users/roles.
- Cookie auth for Admin; JWT/Bearer only if a separate API client needs it.
- Every Admin controller/action: `[Authorize]`. Prefer policies over role-name checks in code.
- Antiforgery on all state-changing Admin POSTs.
- Never store or log plain passwords.

## Roles (starter set)

| Role | Access |
|------|--------|
| `Admin` | Full CMS |
| `Editor` | Content modules (e.g. Blog) |
| `ShopManager` | Shop module |
| `Viewer` | Read-only admin |

Add roles when a module needs them; do not invent ad-hoc role strings in views.

## Policies

Define named policies in one place (e.g. `CMS.Infrastructure` / `DependencyInjection`):

```csharp
options.AddPolicy("AdminOnly", p => p.RequireRole("Admin"));
options.AddPolicy("ManageShop", p => p.RequireRole("Admin", "ShopManager"));
options.AddPolicy("ManageBlog", p => p.RequireRole("Admin", "Editor"));
```

Use:

```csharp
[Authorize(Policy = "ManageShop")]
public class ProductsController : Controller { }
```

## Feature toggles + auth

- Feature off → module routes/UI unavailable (404 or hidden nav).
- Feature on → still require the correct policy.
- Check features with `IFeatureManager`, not `if (shopEnabled)` flags.

```csharp
if (!await _features.IsEnabledAsync("Shop"))
    return NotFound();
```

## UI

- Hide nav items the user cannot access (policy + feature).
- Still enforce authorization on the server for every action.
- Forbidden → consistent Admin error page (Persian), not a stack trace.

## Do Not

- Authorize only in JavaScript or by omitting a sidebar link.
- Put permission logic inside Domain entities.
- Hardcode user IDs or emails for access control.
